#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124308 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124495 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5e2a1713-45a0-5ac9-812e-efe047dbc9eb" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Coordinates of a Vector in a New Basis Given the Change-of-Coordinates Matrix"}] :question/id #uuid "922b0f79-2c87-56f9-bf8a-18711a2a09d7" :question/math-academy-id "e-4838" :question/problem "The change-of-coordinates matrix from the basis $\\mathcal{B}$ to the basis $\\mathcal{C}$ and the coordinates of the vector $\\mathbf{x}$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{B}$ are given below. What is $[\\mathbf{x}]_{\\mathcal{C}}?$ \n\n![](images/bd/bd2cf58c26a51ae147b3714556631e1df786ae6e56cde01f801d9d93f7c258f5.svg)" :question/worked-solution "Given the coordinates of $\\mathbf{x}$ relative to $\\mathcal{B}$ and the change-of-coordinates matrix from $\\mathcal{B}$ to $\\mathcal{C},$ we find the coordinates of $\\mathbf{x}$ relative to $\\mathcal{C}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{C} & ={P}_{B\\to C}[\\,x\\,]_{B} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 6 \\\\ 5 & -8\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124309 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124496 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5ee52fd3-6c79-5016-ade5-46e7bfbb557a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Coordinates of a Vector in a New Basis Given the Inverse Change-of-Coordinates Matrix"}] :question/id #uuid "a3911ca2-a357-5f37-8abc-e01e5518f24b" :question/math-academy-id "e-4840" :question/problem "The change-of-coordinates matrix from $\\mathcal{B}=\\{ \\mathbf{b}_1,\\mathbf{b}_2 \\}$ to $\\mathcal{C} = \\{ \\mathbf{c}_1,\\mathbf{c}_2 \\}$ is given below. Find $[\\mathbf{x}]_{\\mathcal{B}}$ given that $\\mathbf{x} =2\\mathbf{c}_1-6\\mathbf{c}_2.$\n\n![](images/15/15e0d0c04e0352356acab8001936031505931dac7eb7afefa1881dd333448e57.svg)" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change-of-coordinates formula in the form\n\n$$\n[\\,\\mathbf{x}\\,]_{\\mathcal{B}} = P_{\\small\\mathcal{C}\\to\\mathcal{B}} [\\,\\mathbf{x}\\,]_{\\mathcal{C}} .\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to find the change-of-coordinates matrix $P_{\\small\\mathcal{C}\\to\\mathcal{B}}$ from $\\mathcal{C}$ to $\\mathcal{B}.$ To do that, we compute the inverse of $P_{\\small\\mathcal{B}\\to\\mathcal{C}}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{C\\to B} & ={P}_{B\\to C}^{-1} \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 3 \\\\ 6 & -4\\end{aligned}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(-4)(-4)-6\\cdot 3}[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -3 \\\\ -6 & -4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & \\frac{3}{2} \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that since $\\mathbf{x} = 2 \\mathbf{c}_1-6\\mathbf{c}_2,$ we obtain\n![](images/17/178769437e55f1822723ac4cc090efca62aac6be9e3ad9dc8f7971b8a6196484.svg)\n\nNow, we compute $[\\,\\mathbf{x}\\,]_{\\mathcal{B}}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{B} & ={P}_{C\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{C} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & \\frac{3}{2} \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124310 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124497 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7f58bd46-07f7-54a5-8499-5e4e3515b610" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Coordinates of a Vector in a New Basis Given Its Coordinates in the Standard Basis"}] :question/id #uuid "a2d3629f-5b7c-5b65-998f-8754ff11e7b7" :question/math-academy-id "e-4841" :question/problem "The basis $\\mathcal{B}$ of $\\mathbb{R}^2$ and the coordinates of the vector $\\mathbf{x}$ relative to the standard basis $\\mathcal{S}$ are given below. Find $[\\mathbf{x}]_{\\mathcal{B}}.$\n\n![](images/0b/0b395052cc098a6daeb50037d07223b20fb93163c10c31a432c7fb9c9a7804db.svg)" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change-of-coordinates formula in the form\n\n$$\n[\\,\\mathbf{x}\\,]_{\\mathcal{B}} = P_{\\small\\mathcal{S}\\to\\mathcal{B}} [\\,\\mathbf{x}\\,]_{\\mathcal{S}} .\n$$\n\nFirst, recall that the change-of-coordinates matrix from $\\mathcal{B}$ to $\\mathcal{S}$ is the matrix whose columns are the vectors of $\\mathcal{B}$ relative to the standard basis $\\mathcal{S}.$ As a result, we have\n\n![](images/82/82e477164a2708a6f8e7f1142dce3e290d3aa216b5828e0523c16555533f02b7.svg)\n\nNow, we need to find the change-of-coordinates matrix $P_{\\small\\mathcal{S}\\to\\mathcal{B}}$ from $\\mathcal{S}$ to $\\mathcal{B}.$ To do that, we compute the inverse of $P_{\\small\\mathcal{B}\\to\\mathcal{S}}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{S\\to B} & ={P}_{B\\to S}^{-1} \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 10\\end{aligned}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1\\cdot 10-3\\cdot 3}[\\begin{aligned}10 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}10 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute $[\\,\\mathbf{x}\\,]_{\\mathcal{B}}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{B} & ={P}_{S\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{S} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}10 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124384 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124577 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "36f8f02f-acbd-5930-bd0c-8675ac868577" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Coordinates of a Vector in a New Basis Given Its Coordinates in Another Basis"}] :question/id #uuid "f04f55d3-40ec-52a8-a762-d8ac7090b8b8" :question/math-academy-id "e-7276" :question/problem "The bases $\\mathcal{B}$ and $\\mathcal{C}$ of $\\mathbb{R}^2$ and the coordinates of the vector $\\mathbf{x}$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{B}$ are given below. Find $[\\mathbf{x}]_{\\mathcal{C}}.$ \n\n![](images/c6/c61cc387c58af544cf95641a1c2a28e5412d223fb03a7afe5157ee4eba3902bb.svg)" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of $\\mathcal{B}$ and $\\mathcal{C}$ as $\\{ \\mathbf{b}_1,\\mathbf{b}_2 \\}$ and $\\{ \\mathbf{c}_1,\\mathbf{c}_2 \\},$ respectively.\n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from $\\mathcal{B}$ to $\\mathcal{C}$ is a matrix whose columns are the vectors of $\\mathcal{B}$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{C}.$\n\nSo, we need to find the coordinates of $\\mathbf{b}_1$ and $\\mathbf{b}_2$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{C}.$ We can do this simultaneously by considering the matrix\n![](images/76/76d9c82037008323681ec9a0745b39740bf1405c767fc9aaee411cdd1fadde1a.svg)\n\nReducing the left-hand side to reduced row-echelon form using Gaussian elimination, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 6 & 13 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-6){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -5 & -7\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-2){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 11 & 15 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -5 & -7\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the change-of-coordinates matrix $P_{\\small\\mathcal{B}\\to\\mathcal{C}}$ is\n![](images/48/481e6321dd23a8af4c59201685295862cc927731eeec31c122ec62bdf156d179.svg)\n\nFinally, we compute $[\\,\\mathbf{x}\\,]_{\\mathcal{C}}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{C} & ={P}_{B\\to C}[\\,x\\,]_{B} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}11 & 15 \\\\ -5 & -7\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-7 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247373 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124495 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5e2a1713-45a0-5ac9-812e-efe047dbc9eb" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Coordinates of a Vector in a New Basis Given the Change-of-Coordinates Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3dae6502-4e7b-534d-9bd8-c53dee9b8adf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244857} {:answer/id #uuid "5c5339b9-9470-5143-8dcd-ee79f06f692a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245127} {:answer/id #uuid "9927e17c-ce3b-5cba-88cd-822e83219f9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245671} {:answer/id #uuid "bfe775fb-12a8-5495-b1f2-446026491776" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}11 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246022} {:answer/id #uuid "c80894ab-60f5-5cf8-91f2-e04575b9c80a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246084}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5c5339b9-9470-5143-8dcd-ee79f06f692a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245127} :answer-field/id #uuid "a9db8bff-4392-5e61-be0b-6086b5cea1cd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246852}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b0aa2b6d-5bc4-5e57-a220-576badff809b" :question/math-academy-id "q-113557" :question/problem "$$\n{P}_{B\\to C}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -3 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n \n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from the basis $B={{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3}}$ to the basis $C={{c}_{1},{c}_{2},{c}_{3}}$ is given above. Find $[x]_{C}$ given that $x=5{b}_{1}+{b}_{2}-{b}_{3}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that since $x=5{b}_{1}+{b}_{2}-{b}_{3},$ we have\n\n$$\n[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \n\nGiven the coordinates of $x$ relative to $B$ and the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C,$ we find the coordinates of $x$ relative to $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{C} & ={P}_{B\\to C}[\\,x\\,]_{B} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -3 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417187 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124495 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5e2a1713-45a0-5ac9-812e-efe047dbc9eb" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Coordinates of a Vector in a New Basis Given the Change-of-Coordinates Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06f91c85-a6af-54d8-84f8-c73027a4b2f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-35 \\\\ 18\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417125} {:answer/id #uuid "5629c555-bc0f-56b8-91fc-75e26ae8c2ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-17 \\\\ 38\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417137} {:answer/id #uuid "663faa0d-6301-5578-b7ea-57233f7c6d5c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-12 \\\\ 16\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417140} {:answer/id #uuid "8ec023e5-6b5c-5e85-8721-6e3a12e6f471" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-28 \\\\ 38\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417153} {:answer/id #uuid "b210422b-4dd8-58b1-96de-8a7991b12498" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-37 \\\\ 28\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417158}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b210422b-4dd8-58b1-96de-8a7991b12498" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-37 \\\\ 28\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417158} :answer-field/id #uuid "b8fe4383-9008-55d2-ae25-49270558cc7d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417181}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7fe8ffd2-b435-528e-b48e-7a1540a8e35d" :question/math-academy-id "q-113559" :question/problem "The change-of-coordinates matrix from the basis $B$ to the basis $C$ and the coordinates of the vector $x$ relative to the basis $B$ are given below. What is $[x]_{C}?$ \n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to C}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 7 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{aligned}],\\,[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Given the coordinates of $x$ relative to $B$ and the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C,$ we find the coordinates of $x$ relative to $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{C} & ={P}_{B\\to C}[\\,x\\,]_{B} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 7 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-37 \\\\ 28\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417192 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124496 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5ee52fd3-6c79-5016-ade5-46e7bfbb557a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Coordinates of a Vector in a New Basis Given the Inverse Change-of-Coordinates Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1a297d16-52a8-53eb-9dad-1a87b66f4056" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417128} {:answer/id #uuid "67ef9947-0abd-58ce-bfaa-502e27646e6a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417141} {:answer/id #uuid "86daf74f-004a-52d1-918a-94714d07c50d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417148} {:answer/id #uuid "87c5730a-1432-52b5-aaf3-ec874082be11" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417149} {:answer/id #uuid "ed545a0d-bb95-5b28-bd97-fb2a46aed464" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417169}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ed545a0d-bb95-5b28-bd97-fb2a46aed464" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417169} :answer-field/id #uuid "5d503694-4c65-5f5e-80a7-c8e1b24fe6d1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417177}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f0cfdcd8-92d0-5768-aa59-2dc769427d08" :question/math-academy-id "q-113597" :question/problem "The change-of-coordinates matrix from $B={{b}_{1},{b}_{2}}$ to $C={{c}_{1},{c}_{2}}$ is given below. Find $[x]_{B}$ given that $x=4{c}_{1}+6{c}_{2}.$ \n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to C}=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 7 \\\\ 3 & 6\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change-of-coordinates formula in the form\n\n$$\n[\\,x\\,]_{B}={P}_{C\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{C}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to find the change-of-coordinates matrix ${P}_{C\\to B}$ from $C$ to $B.$ To do that, we compute the inverse of ${P}_{B\\to C}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{C\\to B} & ={P}_{B\\to C}^{-1} \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}4 & 7 \\\\ 3 & 6\\end{aligned}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4\\cdot 6-7\\cdot 3}[\\begin{aligned}6 & -7 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -\\frac{7}{3} \\\\ -1 & \\frac{4}{3}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that since $x=4{c}_{1}+6{c}_{2},$ we obtain  \n\n$$\n[x]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we compute $[\\,x\\,]_{B}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{B} & ={P}_{C\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{C} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -\\frac{7}{3} \\\\ -1 & \\frac{4}{3}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417188 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124577 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "36f8f02f-acbd-5930-bd0c-8675ac868577" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Coordinates of a Vector in a New Basis Given Its Coordinates in Another Basis"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3665ab07-2fe3-53da-8bd4-219390934492" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417131} {:answer/id #uuid "88490a55-b515-5dba-8dd0-a984dd302a75" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417150} {:answer/id #uuid "9eeb8242-a2f3-58a1-9c0d-d97cbe1fcdaa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417155} {:answer/id #uuid "ad75aa8f-a4c6-595d-8e09-373c61a5ec3c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417156} {:answer/id #uuid "c69c82e9-2236-526a-ae72-3c203db881f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417164}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9eeb8242-a2f3-58a1-9c0d-d97cbe1fcdaa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417155} :answer-field/id #uuid "ef4b0bd2-0e38-5371-ace1-939725dda131" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417183}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "e28dac56-d247-5873-8ab6-f1ab402c91a9" :question/math-academy-id "q-113612" :question/problem "$$\nB={[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]},\\,C={[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]},\\,[x]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe bases $B$ and $C$ of ${R}^{2}$ and the coordinates of the vector $x$ relative to the basis $C$ are given above. Find $[x]_{B}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of $B$ and $C$ as ${{b}_{1},{b}_{2}}$ and ${{c}_{1},{c}_{2}}$, respectively.\n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from $C$ to $B$ is a matrix whose columns are the vectors of $C$ relative to the basis $B.$\n\nHence, we need to find the coordinates of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ relative to the basis $B.$ We can do this simultaneously by considering the matrix\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | & | \\\\ {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} & {c}_{1} & {c}_{2} \\\\ | & | & | & |\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we simply reduce the left-hand side to reduced row-echelon form using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -2 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=(-\\frac{1}{2}){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-3){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the change-of-coordinates matrix ${P}_{C\\to B}$ is\n\n$$\n{P}_{C\\to B}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute $[\\,x\\,]_{B}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{B} & ={P}_{C\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{C} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247395 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124577 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "36f8f02f-acbd-5930-bd0c-8675ac868577" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Coordinates of a Vector in a New Basis Given Its Coordinates in Another Basis"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d75a68e-8c13-5a08-8a15-00f82421b4b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244435} {:answer/id #uuid "11dbf2ca-3936-56ef-b744-cbeef6b82724" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -8\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244479} {:answer/id #uuid "1df63ca9-e971-572f-a376-2fb9b301ab7b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244575} {:answer/id #uuid "a8793683-8433-5ac4-abb2-dff9688c9320" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245819} {:answer/id #uuid "ccb70fd9-b40d-5c3f-919b-8c5b1a4dcb5c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246136}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a8793683-8433-5ac4-abb2-dff9688c9320" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245819} :answer-field/id #uuid "569ec3c8-6d3d-5836-b3c4-ade9d7c61b67" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246699}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "123986ad-90d2-58aa-b35b-26c97aa5670c" :question/math-academy-id "q-113706" :question/problem "$$\nB={[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]},\\,C={[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]},\\,[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe bases $B$ and $C$ of ${R}^{2}$ and the coordinates of the vector $x$ relative to the basis $B$ are given above. Find $[x]_{C}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of $B$ and $C$ as ${{b}_{1},{b}_{2}}$ and ${{c}_{1},{c}_{2}}$, respectively.\n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C$ is a matrix whose columns are the vectors of $B$ relative to the basis $C.$\n\nHence, we need to find the coordinates of ${b}_{1}$ and ${b}_{2}$ relative to the basis $C.$ We can do this simultaneously by considering the matrix\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | & | \\\\ {c}_{1} & {c}_{2} & {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} \\\\ | & | & | & |\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we simply reduce the left-hand side to reduced row-echelon form using Gaussian elimination\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 6 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-5){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 5 & -6\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-1){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -6 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 5 & -6\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the change-of-coordinates matrix ${P}_{B\\to C}$ is\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to C}=[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 7 \\\\ 5 & -6\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute $[\\,x\\,]_{C}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{C} & ={P}_{B\\to C}[\\,x\\,]_{B} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 7 \\\\ 5 & -6\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124993 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124497 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7f58bd46-07f7-54a5-8499-5e4e3515b610" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Coordinates of a Vector in a New Basis Given Its Coordinates in the Standard Basis"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1dcd4ef1-4949-58ef-aa67-9540954fdc1d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321374} {:answer/id #uuid "41ecfd30-c58f-5e8b-8238-dbc9493bac67" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321670} {:answer/id #uuid "46420e90-1c10-5b3a-a995-8806f1bcee0b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321702} {:answer/id #uuid "6e560467-13af-562b-ad43-d89f3b3f3260" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322002} {:answer/id #uuid "d1bf20ff-37e7-5af1-a632-1f6b46091107" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322785}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "46420e90-1c10-5b3a-a995-8806f1bcee0b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321702} :answer-field/id #uuid "4370689f-d468-5230-a5de-f45535b13a11" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323298}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9b5f682f-a0de-52f6-930d-b54c3a22eede" :question/math-academy-id "q-36488" :question/problem "$$\nB={[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ -8\\end{aligned}]},\\,[x]_{S}=[\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe basis $B$ of ${R}^{2}$ and the coordinates of the vector $x$ relative to the standard basis $S$ are given above. Find $[x]_{B}.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change-of-coordinates formula in the form\n\n$$\n[\\,x\\,]_{B}={P}_{S\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{S}.\n$$\n\nFirst, recall that the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $S$ is the matrix whose columns are the vectors of $B$ relative to the standard basis $S.$ As a result, we have\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to S}=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 9 \\\\ -2 & -8\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the change-of-coordinates matrix ${P}_{S\\to B}$ from $S$ to $B.$ To do that, we compute the inverse of ${P}_{B\\to S}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{S\\to B} & ={P}_{B\\to S}^{-1} \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}3 & 9 \\\\ -2 & -8\\end{aligned}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3\\cdot (-8)-9\\cdot (-2)}[\\begin{aligned}-8 & -9 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{3} & \\frac{3}{2} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{3} & -\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute $[\\,x\\,]_{B}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{B} & ={P}_{S\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{S} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{3} & \\frac{3}{2} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{3} & -\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417186 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124495 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5e2a1713-45a0-5ac9-812e-efe047dbc9eb" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Coordinates of a Vector in a New Basis Given the Change-of-Coordinates Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "229988f7-658e-531e-a75e-4a633a968a80" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-12 \\\\ 20\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417129} {:answer/id #uuid "61abc92b-674d-5626-b141-2ec57981f70d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-22 \\\\ 21\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417139} {:answer/id #uuid "78245600-a1f8-5a21-88bd-e06c505e5fc9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-12 \\\\ 11\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417144} {:answer/id #uuid "8d3b91f8-ceab-5fd2-bfae-9dc1cd0ce79f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-22 \\\\ 12\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417152} {:answer/id #uuid "b8fb5f8b-a5f6-5906-85ff-1e2f8a50b96f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-21 \\\\ 22\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417159}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "61abc92b-674d-5626-b141-2ec57981f70d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-22 \\\\ 21\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417139} :answer-field/id #uuid "b04e8c67-5c25-59a1-9e4d-4e87a86fbc03" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417180}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3ce9ec8d-1b85-5d4c-b1b5-270c31bca4d5" :question/math-academy-id "q-36500" :question/problem "The change-of-coordinates matrix from the basis $B$ to the basis $C$ and the coordinates of the vector $x$ relative to the basis $B$ are given below. What is $[x]_{C}?$ \n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to C}=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -8 \\\\ -5 & 3\\end{aligned}],\\,[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Given the coordinates of $x$ relative to $B$ and the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C,$ we find the coordinates of $x$ relative to $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{C} & ={P}_{B\\to C}[\\,x\\,]_{B} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -8 \\\\ -5 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-22 \\\\ 21\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124994 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124496 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5ee52fd3-6c79-5016-ade5-46e7bfbb557a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Coordinates of a Vector in a New Basis Given the Inverse Change-of-Coordinates Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b3218afa-c5a1-5e45-b2a7-29995b720c79" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}12 \\\\ 27\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391999} {:answer/id #uuid "f25974c9-ca87-5bf9-957d-8b53cd37be8d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392000} {:answer/id #uuid "48c64c3a-7f08-535f-a11d-91e574947824" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ 22\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392001} {:answer/id #uuid "cf0a32bf-78a6-511f-9f75-0ac4d9b7caeb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}24 \\\\ 30\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392002} {:answer/id #uuid "81442015-4c62-5e1f-9ad1-d16a4137f7f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}22 \\\\ 35\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392003}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "81442015-4c62-5e1f-9ad1-d16a4137f7f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}22 \\\\ 35\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392003} :answer-field/id #uuid "fa84e0e3-e3ca-5864-9c75-6d5148345ac3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391998}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f503090a-50ea-5c09-941f-e82b557a90db" :question/math-academy-id "q-36503" :question/problem "The change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C$ and the coordinates of the vector $x$ relative to the basis $C$ are given below. What is $[x]_{B}?$ \n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to C}=[\\begin{aligned}5 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{aligned}],\\,[x]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change-of-coordinates formula in the form\n\n$$\n[\\,x\\,]_{B}={P}_{C\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{C}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to find the change-of-coordinates matrix ${P}_{C\\to B}$ from $C$ to $B.$ To do that, we compute the inverse of ${P}_{B\\to C}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{C\\to B} & ={P}_{B\\to C}^{-1} \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}5 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{aligned}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5\\cdot 2-(-3)\\cdot (-3)}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute $[\\,x\\,]_{B}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{B} & ={P}_{C\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{C} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 5\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}22 \\\\ 35\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323887 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124495 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5e2a1713-45a0-5ac9-812e-efe047dbc9eb" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Coordinates of a Vector in a New Basis Given the Change-of-Coordinates Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00f62d0f-822f-5dc7-97c9-7bf26ef3dbe6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -14 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321157} {:answer/id #uuid "211d97a5-508e-5739-b4e0-19bdd50e3bf3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321395} {:answer/id #uuid "5126e114-bfbf-59a0-a5dd-983b59417e32" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -18 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321785} {:answer/id #uuid "84c4e861-48eb-548a-95ad-553b6de5abf0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -10 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322196} {:answer/id #uuid "a8da0b30-6b44-5bcb-9e2f-99b6c4ad1e0c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -7 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322473}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "00f62d0f-822f-5dc7-97c9-7bf26ef3dbe6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -14 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321157} :answer-field/id #uuid "0f527b5a-91ac-5397-988a-c8c887f42b2a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323198}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "41491635-98e5-5537-8640-d445d0545026" :question/math-academy-id "q-37152" :question/problem "$$\n{P}_{B\\to C}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 7 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n \n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from the basis $B={{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3}}$ to the basis $C={{c}_{1},{c}_{2},{c}_{3}}$ is given above. Find $[x]_{C}$ given that $x=4{b}_{3}-2{b}_{1}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that since $x=4{b}_{3}-2{b}_{1},$ we have\n\n$$\n[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \n\nGiven the coordinates of $x$ relative to $B$ and the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C,$ we find the coordinates of $x$ relative to $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{C} & ={P}_{B\\to C}[\\,x\\,]_{B} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 7 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -14 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125036 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124497 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7f58bd46-07f7-54a5-8499-5e4e3515b610" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Coordinates of a Vector in a New Basis Given Its Coordinates in the Standard Basis"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7b3fc777-c87e-5494-9361-753fc99b8a92" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417145} {:answer/id #uuid "af2e1656-5790-518b-ae95-17ddec42b6bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417157} {:answer/id #uuid "c820d187-6507-5a27-8119-ca85d381bd74" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417165} {:answer/id #uuid "d1949552-5c2a-51ed-85be-630d9f8df6a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417166} {:answer/id #uuid "fc368894-a420-5e78-a8ce-445dfc038e7c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417173}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d1949552-5c2a-51ed-85be-630d9f8df6a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417166} :answer-field/id #uuid "77968fba-758e-5c47-b717-abe8d60fbc59" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417179}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "618e1711-d591-5872-b815-9d14ad33b674" :question/math-academy-id "q-37160" :question/problem "$$\nB={[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 16\\end{aligned}]},\\,[x]_{S}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe basis $B$ of ${R}^{2}$ and the coordinates of the vector $x$ relative to the standard basis $S$ are given above. Find $[x]_{B}.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change-of-coordinates formula in the form\n\n$$\n[\\,x\\,]_{B}={P}_{S\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{S}.\n$$\n\nFirst, recall that the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $S$ is the matrix whose columns are the vectors of $B$ relative to the standard basis $S.$ As a result, we have\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to S}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 \\\\ 3 & 16\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the change-of-coordinates matrix ${P}_{S\\to B}$ from $S$ to $B.$ To do that, we compute the inverse of ${P}_{B\\to S}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{S\\to B} & ={P}_{B\\to S}^{-1} \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 \\\\ 3 & 16\\end{aligned}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1\\cdot 16-3\\cdot 5}[\\begin{aligned}16 & -5 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}16 & -5 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute $[\\,x\\,]_{B}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{B} & ={P}_{S\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{S} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}16 & -5 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417185 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124496 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5ee52fd3-6c79-5016-ade5-46e7bfbb557a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Coordinates of a Vector in a New Basis Given the Inverse Change-of-Coordinates Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5c1b7743-c305-5999-a721-23098449b1cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417138} {:answer/id #uuid "76daf001-db03-5b02-b5ce-d1bdb4a9261e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417143} {:answer/id #uuid "848c95d9-4948-5e5b-b405-b76d8bb0404e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417146} {:answer/id #uuid "e45951f9-b2ac-5925-8dca-6a0e9e74ecc4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-8 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417167} {:answer/id #uuid "e5ad6413-b74c-561a-9d52-2593718ee8e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-7 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417168}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e45951f9-b2ac-5925-8dca-6a0e9e74ecc4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-8 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417167} :answer-field/id #uuid "259091c3-ae0d-594d-b51a-a8e034136037" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417174}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2be7f5e5-3b31-5fb7-85df-a4fc0339bd35" :question/math-academy-id "q-82598" :question/problem "The change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C$ and the coordinates of the vector $x$ relative to the basis $C$ are given below. What is $[x]_{B}?$ \n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to C}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 \\\\ 3 & -4\\end{aligned}],\\,[x]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change-of-coordinates formula in the form\n\n$$\n[\\,x\\,]_{B}={P}_{C\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{C}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to find the change-of-coordinates matrix ${P}_{C\\to B}$ from $C$ to $B.$ To do that, we compute the inverse of ${P}_{B\\to C}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{C\\to B} & ={P}_{B\\to C}^{-1} \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 \\\\ 3 & -4\\end{aligned}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(-2)\\cdot (-4)-3\\cdot 2}[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -2 \\\\ -3 & -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -1 \\\\ -\\frac{3}{2} & -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute $[\\,x\\,]_{B}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{B} & ={P}_{C\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{C} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -1 \\\\ -\\frac{3}{2} & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-8 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351438 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124496 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5ee52fd3-6c79-5016-ade5-46e7bfbb557a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Coordinates of a Vector in a New Basis Given the Inverse Change-of-Coordinates Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6d08356c-7931-5cd9-9d11-4c39181be760" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349451} {:answer/id #uuid "9af31c0d-f620-5346-8cc8-0520d3750ee8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-9 \\\\ -8\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349828} {:answer/id #uuid "d7bf9bfb-32be-5f38-ad4d-61f15bcaba54" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350336} {:answer/id #uuid "e73a838e-f880-5ec9-8ca6-4f2bee27ab24" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350469} {:answer/id #uuid "f4ff9120-1464-5615-9c5e-ff1e12550f88" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350589}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6d08356c-7931-5cd9-9d11-4c39181be760" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349451} :answer-field/id #uuid "ddf5d9a8-e075-5599-8957-df3a4a61095c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351095}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1a8774bf-f531-5684-a4ee-be071e1e5ed5" :question/math-academy-id "q-82599" :question/problem "The change-of-coordinates matrix from $B={{b}_{1},{b}_{2}}$ to $C={{c}_{1},{c}_{2}}$ is given below. Find $[x]_{B}$ given that $x=3{c}_{1}-3{c}_{2}.$\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to C}=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -5 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change-of-coordinates formula in the form\n\n$$\n[\\,x\\,]_{B}={P}_{C\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{C}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to find the change-of-coordinates matrix ${P}_{C\\to B}$ from $C$ to $B.$ To do that, we compute the inverse of ${P}_{B\\to C}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{C\\to B} & ={P}_{B\\to C}^{-1} \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}2 & -5 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\cdot 3-(-1)\\cdot (-5)}[\\begin{aligned}3 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that since $x=3{c}_{1}-3{c}_{2},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n[x]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we compute $[\\,x\\,]_{B}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{B} & ={P}_{C\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{C} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394414 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124497 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7f58bd46-07f7-54a5-8499-5e4e3515b610" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Coordinates of a Vector in a New Basis Given Its Coordinates in the Standard Basis"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9b1e14eb-c6fe-5d5e-a0dc-b061059559c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394416} {:answer/id #uuid "985b430f-4b57-5849-b48f-23adc660de97" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394417} {:answer/id #uuid "dab5b566-c168-514d-b3c6-1c83c5b47cd0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394418} {:answer/id #uuid "a6a4ab04-a137-58d3-a387-4f383d0ae878" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394419} {:answer/id #uuid "71cfe1b4-e7cb-5edf-9ce0-5c9a0fe890fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394420}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "985b430f-4b57-5849-b48f-23adc660de97" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394417} :answer-field/id #uuid "e7cbaef8-14a8-5424-bff9-cdd4ca81b618" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394415}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5c7d63da-59ab-5308-9b89-bf650fb9db9b" :question/math-academy-id "q-82606" :question/problem "$$\nB={[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]},\\,[x]_{S}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe basis $B$ of ${R}^{2}$ and the coordinates of the vector $x$ relative to the standard basis $S$ are given above. Find $[x]_{B}.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change-of-coordinates formula in the form\n\n$$\n[\\,x\\,]_{B}={P}_{S\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{S}.\n$$\n\nFirst, recall that the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $S$ is the matrix whose columns are the vectors of $B$ relative to the standard basis $S.$ As a result, we have\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to S}=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 9 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the change-of-coordinates matrix ${P}_{S\\to B}$ from $S$ to $B.$ To do that, we compute the inverse of ${P}_{B\\to S}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{S\\to B} & ={P}_{B\\to S}^{-1} \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}5 & 9 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5\\cdot 2-1\\cdot 9}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -9 \\\\ -1 & 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -9 \\\\ -1 & 5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute $[\\,x\\,]_{B}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{B} & ={P}_{S\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{S} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -9 \\\\ -1 & 5\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394421 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124497 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7f58bd46-07f7-54a5-8499-5e4e3515b610" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Coordinates of a Vector in a New Basis Given Its Coordinates in the Standard Basis"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "84bf4f13-8394-5e74-8611-52bcbe63b443" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394423} {:answer/id #uuid "a4f43405-b85b-5f16-ae6f-5e4abfc15221" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394424} {:answer/id #uuid "1be8e3f6-f794-5e2a-9dc7-026e640b732f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}12 \\\\ -17\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394425} {:answer/id #uuid "924d16bd-723c-52e2-9669-dce0fa52dce9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-10 \\\\ -18\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394426} {:answer/id #uuid "80c96893-628f-5b23-b717-b15529348c04" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394427}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "924d16bd-723c-52e2-9669-dce0fa52dce9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-10 \\\\ -18\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394426} :answer-field/id #uuid "4dc08976-f574-52b6-a28f-57c50285b1e0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394422}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "13aef9df-8682-5fe0-a906-e0817933e236" :question/math-academy-id "q-82609" :question/problem "$$\nB={[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]},\\,[x]_{S}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe basis $B$ of ${R}^{2}$ and the coordinates of the vector $x$ relative to the standard basis $S$ are given above. Find $[x]_{B}.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change-of-coordinates formula in the form\n\n$$\n[\\,x\\,]_{B}={P}_{S\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{S}.\n$$\n\nFirst, recall that the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $S$ is the matrix whose columns are the vectors of $B$ relative to the standard basis $S.$ As a result, we have\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to S}=[\\begin{aligned}7 & -4 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the change-of-coordinates matrix ${P}_{S\\to B}$ from $S$ to $B.$ To do that, we compute the inverse of ${P}_{B\\to S}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{S\\to B} & ={P}_{B\\to S}^{-1} \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}7 & -4 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7\\cdot 1-(-2)\\cdot (-4)}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 7\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -4 \\\\ -2 & -7\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute $[\\,x\\,]_{B}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{B} & ={P}_{S\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{S} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -4 \\\\ -2 & -7\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-10 \\\\ -18\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351443 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124577 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "36f8f02f-acbd-5930-bd0c-8675ac868577" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Coordinates of a Vector in a New Basis Given Its Coordinates in Another Basis"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5a356bf6-c5f6-5626-b37d-d3aa5d04ec26" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-11 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349277} {:answer/id #uuid "9d1ff8f8-bc39-524a-8272-e771ca3767b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}18 \\\\ -29\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349849} {:answer/id #uuid "bf4fcc3c-344a-5fe2-a3f1-717dc5d18898" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350145} {:answer/id #uuid "cbcd859b-049f-5e51-885f-b10217b48fcb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350241} {:answer/id #uuid "cd9f3522-d091-5f2a-a8c4-a46fc74e67fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-10 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350256}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5a356bf6-c5f6-5626-b37d-d3aa5d04ec26" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-11 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349277} :answer-field/id #uuid "0fa41aa0-25a9-5a0a-b9d6-671a033593b9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350735}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "77f1fca8-2c83-502e-92f3-c4632be6ced3" :question/math-academy-id "q-82618" :question/problem "$$\nB={[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}]},\\,C={[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]},\\,[x]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe bases $B$ and $C$ of ${R}^{2}$ and the coordinates of the vector $x$ relative to the basis $C$ are given above. Find $[x]_{B}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of $B$ and $C$ as ${{b}_{1},{b}_{2}}$ and ${{c}_{1},{c}_{2}}$, respectively.\n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from $C$ to $B$ is a matrix whose columns are the vectors of $C$ relative to the basis $B.$\n\nHence, we need to find the coordinates of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ relative to the basis $B.$ We can do this simultaneously by considering the matrix\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | & | \\\\ {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} & {c}_{1} & {c}_{2} \\\\ | & | & | & |\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we simply reduce the left-hand side to reduced row-echelon form using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 3 & -2 \\\\ 3 & 10 & 4 & 3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 3 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -5 & 9\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-3){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 18 & -29 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -5 & 9\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the change-of-coordinates matrix ${P}_{C\\to B}$ is\n\n$$\n{P}_{C\\to B}=[\\begin{aligned}18 & -29 \\\\ -5 & 9\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute $[\\,x\\,]_{B}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{B} & ={P}_{C\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{C} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}18 & -29 \\\\ -5 & 9\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-11 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417191 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124577 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "36f8f02f-acbd-5930-bd0c-8675ac868577" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Coordinates of a Vector in a New Basis Given Its Coordinates in Another Basis"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00337181-d246-5d62-ad84-bc4061bb4096" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417124} {:answer/id #uuid "3bc312ec-9a5e-5eb2-8bb9-a8936c73c9ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417132} {:answer/id #uuid "4e2646f8-d294-5f71-920f-5fb9383c0ce2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417135} {:answer/id #uuid "8c4a3883-bbae-5787-8a87-5357e57022a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417151} {:answer/id #uuid "c2f56259-99a1-5f0e-acbf-9e1fb79a1fc7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-9 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417163}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "00337181-d246-5d62-ad84-bc4061bb4096" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417124} :answer-field/id #uuid "4b4d2688-b184-5811-a90b-5873e7a47367" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417175}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ef5b0192-0a59-538e-b7e8-f10e0dd5b78e" :question/math-academy-id "q-82621" :question/problem "$$\nB={[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 16\\end{aligned}]},\\,C={[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]},\\,[x]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe bases $B$ and $C$ of ${R}^{2}$ and the coordinates of the vector $x$ relative to the basis $C$ are given above. Find $[x]_{B}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of $B$ and $C$ as ${{b}_{1},{b}_{2}}$ and ${{c}_{1},{c}_{2}}$, respectively.\n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from $C$ to $B$ is a matrix whose columns are the vectors of $C$ relative to the basis $B.$\n\nHence, we need to find the coordinates of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ relative to the basis $B.$ We can do this simultaneously by considering the matrix\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | & | \\\\ {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} & {c}_{1} & {c}_{2} \\\\ | & | & | & |\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we simply reduce the left-hand side to reduced row-echelon form using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 16 & 1 & -2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-5){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -4 & -2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-3){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 13 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -4 & -2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the change-of-coordinates matrix ${P}_{C\\to B}$ is\n\n$$\n{P}_{C\\to B}=[\\begin{aligned}13 & 6 \\\\ -4 & -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute $[\\,x\\,]_{B}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{B} & ={P}_{C\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{C} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}13 & 6 \\\\ -4 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417190 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124495 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5e2a1713-45a0-5ac9-812e-efe047dbc9eb" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Coordinates of a Vector in a New Basis Given the Change-of-Coordinates Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "19c523e9-7f45-5159-8b99-5f289dc80d0e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417127} {:answer/id #uuid "51c298fd-e567-5dcc-b831-f04003f2f4db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 10 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417136} {:answer/id #uuid "85f7a332-11d7-56c1-af54-08f89b7cdbb8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417147} {:answer/id #uuid "9968b0a6-1e90-5008-8fc1-0d8dbdcacf08" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -15\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417154} {:answer/id #uuid "bdcaea72-2ad3-5720-8e68-209ab0dd89b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 11 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417160}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "51c298fd-e567-5dcc-b831-f04003f2f4db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 10 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417136} :answer-field/id #uuid "dda3a792-a5c1-587a-9edb-ce17ef59f2fa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417182}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e866909b-8838-5c06-a42c-cac8cb9d71e5" :question/math-academy-id "q-82703" :question/problem "$$\n{P}_{B\\to C}=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -4 \\\\ -3 & -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n \n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from the basis $B={{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3}}$ to the basis $C={{c}_{1},{c}_{2},{c}_{3}}$ is given above. Find $[x]_{C}$ given that $x=4{b}_{2}-2{b}_{1}-3{b}_{3}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that since $x=4{b}_{2}-2{b}_{1}-3{b}_{3},$ we have\n\n$$\n[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \n\nGiven the coordinates of $x$ relative to $B$ and the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C,$ we find the coordinates of $x$ relative to $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{C} & ={P}_{B\\to C}[\\,x\\,]_{B} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -4 \\\\ -3 & -2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 10 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394442 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124496 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5ee52fd3-6c79-5016-ade5-46e7bfbb557a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Coordinates of a Vector in a New Basis Given the Inverse Change-of-Coordinates Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "549a0eee-e056-5b3e-945c-dc08eab7d67e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394444} {:answer/id #uuid "780e5eeb-967c-58e6-9b97-e28954821893" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394445} {:answer/id #uuid "5fbfa154-65b9-54ed-a535-feab787fda53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394446} {:answer/id #uuid "b9d33501-64aa-5fb9-b7f1-cd2d776eee3e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394447} {:answer/id #uuid "48d8e050-c67b-591d-a02d-6b6dede8752f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ -8\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394448}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5fbfa154-65b9-54ed-a535-feab787fda53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394446} :answer-field/id #uuid "e2130ea6-8c98-546a-8865-99cd9a3a8e9c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394443}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3a7ee4f4-73c6-5824-a327-bfe7ce4e304c" :question/math-academy-id "q-82707" :question/problem "The change-of-coordinates matrix from $B={{b}_{1},{b}_{2}}$ to $C={{c}_{1},{c}_{2}}$ is given below. Find $[x]_{B}$ given that $x=5{c}_{1}-{c}_{2}.$\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to C}=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 10 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change-of-coordinates formula in the form\n\n$$\n[\\,x\\,]_{B}={P}_{C\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{C}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to find the change-of-coordinates matrix ${P}_{C\\to B}$ from $C$ to $B.$ To do that, we compute the inverse of ${P}_{B\\to C}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{C\\to B} & ={P}_{B\\to C}^{-1} \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}5 & 10 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{aligned}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5\\cdot 5-2\\cdot 10}[\\begin{aligned}5 & -10 \\\\ -2 & 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ -\\frac{2}{5} & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that since $x=5{c}_{1}-{c}_{2},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n[x]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we compute $[\\,x\\,]_{B}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{B} & ={P}_{C\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{C} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ -\\frac{2}{5} & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417184 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124497 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7f58bd46-07f7-54a5-8499-5e4e3515b610" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Coordinates of a Vector in a New Basis Given Its Coordinates in the Standard Basis"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "339eb9d7-c3ff-5722-b2a4-62c01e506dfe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417130} {:answer/id #uuid "7545326f-2ed3-5b45-b83f-fdce64ffd43b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417142} {:answer/id #uuid "c0ab3da6-d52a-52fe-8824-525245387581" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417162} {:answer/id #uuid "f99a0a3e-8e69-5c5a-8d55-fb74464b4d49" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417171} {:answer/id #uuid "fa770ac4-af40-5a3c-b0e8-fb5ff74934d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417172}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fa770ac4-af40-5a3c-b0e8-fb5ff74934d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417172} :answer-field/id #uuid "61a8f832-f2b3-5123-8233-870da3bb21e1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417178}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "15cdd9a6-5092-51ed-93a5-b2dd11d2ff12" :question/math-academy-id "q-82713" :question/problem "$$\nB={[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]},\\,[x]_{S}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe basis $B$ of ${R}^{2}$ and the coordinates of the vector $x$ relative to the standard basis $S$ are given above. Find $[x]_{B}.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change-of-coordinates formula in the form\n\n$$\n[\\,x\\,]_{B}={P}_{S\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{S}.\n$$\n\nFirst, recall that the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $S$ is the matrix whose columns are the vectors of $B$ relative to the standard basis $S.$ As a result, we have\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to S}=[\\begin{aligned}7 & 5 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the change-of-coordinates matrix ${P}_{S\\to B}$ from $S$ to $B.$ To do that, we compute the inverse of ${P}_{B\\to S}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{S\\to B} & ={P}_{B\\to S}^{-1} \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}7 & 5 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7\\cdot 2-3\\cdot 5}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -5 \\\\ -3 & 7\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 5 \\\\ 3 & -7\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute $[\\,x\\,]_{B}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{B} & ={P}_{S\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{S} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 5 \\\\ 3 & -7\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417189 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124577 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "36f8f02f-acbd-5930-bd0c-8675ac868577" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Coordinates of a Vector in a New Basis Given Its Coordinates in Another Basis"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "164c778b-5b14-5603-bf90-827e8830f635" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417126} {:answer/id #uuid "478f5fce-6266-5067-9a1b-4f2ed288f0d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417133} {:answer/id #uuid "4c7f10ae-9ffa-596d-a2d2-99ebac94264d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -16\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417134} {:answer/id #uuid "bf4e4798-2fc8-5413-93d4-21fffdbf8a55" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417161} {:answer/id #uuid "f2cec5d4-fb73-55ac-9a1a-3d5575086234" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417170}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4c7f10ae-9ffa-596d-a2d2-99ebac94264d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -16\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417134} :answer-field/id #uuid "4c43a46c-e182-5838-aaf6-58e012185c17" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417176}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "e6749d20-907b-5109-a739-bd517d7b2b94" :question/math-academy-id "q-82717" :question/problem "$$\nB={[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]},\\,C={[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]},\\,[x]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe bases $B$ and $C$ of ${R}^{2}$ and the coordinates of the vector $x$ relative to the basis $C$ are given above. Find $[x]_{B}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of $B$ and $C$ as ${{b}_{1},{b}_{2}}$ and ${{c}_{1},{c}_{2}}$, respectively.\n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from $C$ to $B$ is a matrix whose columns are the vectors of $C$ relative to the basis $B.$\n\nHence, we need to find the coordinates of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ relative to the basis $B.$ We can do this simultaneously by considering the matrix\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | & | \\\\ {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} & {c}_{1} & {c}_{2} \\\\ | & | & | & |\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we simply reduce the left-hand side to reduced row-echelon form using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 1 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 1 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-4){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -9 & -4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the change-of-coordinates matrix ${P}_{C\\to B}$ is\n\n$$\n{P}_{C\\to B}=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ -9 & -4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute $[\\,x\\,]_{B}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{B} & ={P}_{C\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{C} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ -9 & -4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -16\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
